{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"cat pose-mcp/internal/pose/material_binding.go | head -75; nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_scope.go | sed -n '137,198p'; sed -n '155,173p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry_test.go; rg -n '\\\"(program|args|name)\\\"' .pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json | tail -20; cat .pose/rules/backend-go.md\"","aggregated_output":"package pose\n\nimport (\n\t\"crypto/sha256\"\n\t\"encoding/hex\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"path\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"strings\"\n)\n\n// Validation evidence binds to the material it validated (spec\n// pose-validation-evidence-material-binding, ADR\n// validation-evidence-binds-to-validated-material). A commit whose every\n// changed path is a derived administrative record does not change what the\n// checks exercised, so it does not move the material digest.\n\n// engineVersion is the running engine, registered by the binaries (internal/pose\n// stays importable on its own). It is part of every material digest, so a new\n// engine never inherits evidence the previous one produced.\nvar engineVersion = \"unregistered\"\n\n// SetEngineVersion registers the running engine for material digests.\nfunc SetEngineVersion(v string) { engineVersion = v }\n\n// administrativeDirs is the closed list of derived-record directories. It is\n// owned by the engine; adding a class is a contract change that needs an ADR.\nvar administrativeDirs = []string{\".pose/indexes/\", \".pose/results/\", \".pose/state/\", \".pose/assessments/\"}\n\n// materialIndexes are files inside an administrative directory that still\n// change what the checks demand.\nvar materialIndexes = map[string]bool{\".pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\": true}\n\n// IsAdministrativePath reports whether p is a derived administrative record.\nfunc IsAdministrativePath(p string) bool {\n\tif p == \"\" || p != path.Clean(p) || strings.HasPrefix(p, \"/\") || strings.HasPrefix(p, \"../\") {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\tif materialIndexes[p] {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\tif !recordExtensions[strings.ToLower(path.Ext(p))] {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\tfor _, dir := range administrativeDirs {\n\t\tif strings.HasPrefix(p, dir) {\n\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif rest, ok := strings.CutPrefix(p, \".pose/\"); ok {\n\t\tif i := strings.Index(rest, \"/\"); i > 0 && strings.HasPrefix(rest[:i], \"review-\") {\n\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false\n}\n\n// recordExtensions are the data formats a derived record is written in. Code or\n// a script stored in a class is material: that keeps a source file moved or\n// copied into a class from hiding its later edits. A copied data file stays\n// indistinguishable from a record; a check that reads data out of a derived-record\n// directory is outside this contract (ADR validation-evidence-binds-to-validated-material).\nvar recordExtensions = map[string]bool{\".json\": true, \".jsonl\": true, \".ndjson\": true, \".md\": true, \".txt\": true, \".log\": true, \".transcript\": true}\n\n// TreeEntry is one entry of the validated head's tree.\ntype TreeEntry struct{ Mode, Blob string }\n\nfunc regularMode(mode string) bool { return mode == \"100644\" || mode == \"100755\" }\n\n// MaterialInputs are what a material digest is computed from: the paths a change\n// set names (both sides of a rename, and the validation matrix) and the\n// destinations of renames. They are stored with the set so the digest can be\n// recomputed against the current tree without the commits' own records.\ntype MaterialInputs struct {\n   137\t\t\t\t\tif len(m.Rel) > len(match) {\n   138\t\t\t\t\t\tmatch = m.Rel\n   139\t\t\t\t\t}\n   140\t\t\t\t}\n   141\t\t\t}\n   142\t\t\treturn match\n   143\t\t}\n   144\t\t// Every changed file selects, independently of one another (review rounds\n   145\t\t// 2–4, N2, N5, N7, N8):\n   146\t\t//   - its most specific owning module;\n   147\t\t//   - every module under it (a gitlink or a replaced directory changes what\n   148\t\t//     lies beneath);\n   149\t\t//   - every module whose declared dependsOn contains it, lies under it, or\n   150\t\t//     is the project root \".\", whether or not the dependency is discovered;\n   151\t\t//   - when no module owns it: the root module and every module that does\n   152\t\t//     not declare itself self-contained (safe execution wins).\n   153\t\tselectModule := func(rel, reason string) {\n   154\t\t\tif _, ok := sel.Selected[rel]; !ok {\n   155\t\t\t\tsel.Selected[rel] = reason\n   156\t\t\t}\n   157\t\t}\n   158\t\tfor _, file := range sel.Changed {\n   159\t\t\towner := moduleOf(file)\n   160\t\t\tif owner != \"\" {\n   161\t\t\t\tselectModule(owner, \"contains changed file: \"+file)\n   162\t\t\t}\n   163\t\t\tfor _, m := range modules {\n   164\t\t\t\tif m.Rel != \".\" && strings.HasPrefix(m.Rel, file+\"/\") {\n   165\t\t\t\t\tselectModule(m.Rel, \"changed path above the module: \"+file)\n   166\t\t\t\t}\n   167\t\t\t\t// Every eligible dependency counts, whatever its order (review\n   168\t\t\t\t// round 5, N11).\n   169\t\t\t\t// A dependency that is the file's own owner reaches its dependents\n   170\t\t\t\t// through the transitive widening; any other one, discovered or not,\n   171\t\t\t\t// selects here (review round 6, N15).\n   172\t\t\t\tfor _, dep := range declaredDependencyHits(file, meta.Modules[m.Rel].DependsOn) {\n   173\t\t\t\t\tif dep != owner {\n   174\t\t\t\t\t\tselectModule(m.Rel, \"declared dependency \"+dep+\" changed: \"+file)\n   175\t\t\t\t\t\tbreak\n   176\t\t\t\t\t}\n   177\t\t\t\t}\n   178\t\t\t\tif owner == \"\" && (m.Rel == \".\" || !meta.Modules[m.Rel].SelfContained) {\n   179\t\t\t\t\tselectModule(m.Rel, \"root-level change outside any module: \"+file)\n   180\t\t\t\t}\n   181\t\t\t}\n   182\t\t}\n   183\t\t// Policy widening: high-criticality modules always run.\n   184\t\tfor _, m := range modules {\n   185\t\t\tif meta.Modules[m.Rel].Criticality == \"high\" {\n   186\t\t\t\tif _, ok := sel.Selected[m.Rel]; !ok {\n   187\t\t\t\t\tsel.Selected[m.Rel] = \"policy: criticality high always runs\"\n   188\t\t\t\t}\n   189\t\t\t}\n   190\t\t}\n   191\t\t// Transitive dependents of selected modules (declared edges only).\n   192\t\tfor changed := true; changed; {\n   193\t\t\tchanged = false\n   194\t\t\tfor _, m := range modules {\n   195\t\t\t\tif _, ok := sel.Selected[m.Rel]; ok {\n   196\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   197\t\t\t\t}\n   198\t\t\t\tfor _, dep := range meta.Modules[m.Rel].DependsOn {\n\nfunc TestCarryKeepsTheOriginalHeadAcrossChainedCarries(t *testing.T) {\n\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n\tbase := f.baseline()\n\tf.write(\"a/one.go\", \"package a\\n\")\n\tf.commit(\"change a\")\n\tif code, out, _ := f.validate(\"--carry\"); code != 0 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"exit=%d %s\", code, out)\n\t}\n\tf.commit(\"record again\")\n\tf.write(\"a/two.go\", \"package a\\n\")\n\tf.commit(\"change a again\")\n\t_, _, run := f.validate(\"--carry\")\n\tif b := checkOf(run, \"b\"); b.CarriedFrom != base {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"b carried from %q, want the head it ran at %s\", b.CarriedFrom, base)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestCarryKeepsACarriedFailureAFailure(t *testing.T) {\n2986:          \"program\": \"go\",\n2987:          \"args\": [\n2999:          \"name\": \"delegated-approval-survives-routine-drift-integration\",\n3000:          \"program\": \"go\",\n3001:          \"args\": [\n3013:          \"name\": \"help-lists-every-command-integration\",\n3014:          \"program\": \"go\",\n3015:          \"args\": [\n3027:          \"name\": \"review-brief-conclusion-rules-integration\",\n3028:          \"program\": \"go\",\n3029:          \"args\": [\n3041:          \"name\": \"done-spec-keeps-its-sealed-plan-integration\",\n3042:          \"program\": \"go\",\n3043:          \"args\": [\n3056:          \"name\": \"common-domain-and-cli-mcp-handoff-integration\",\n3057:          \"program\": \"go\",\n3058:          \"args\": [\n3072:          \"name\": \"generation-bound-query-cursors-integration\",\n3073:          \"program\": \"go\",\n3074:          \"args\": [\n# Rule: Backend Go\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for HTTP handlers, domain services, persistence, concurrency, and integrations between Go services.\n\n## Required patterns\n\n- Handle and propagate errors with enough context for diagnosis.\n- Validate handler input and return HTTP status codes consistent with the contract.\n- Keep business rules in service or domain layers, not in transport code.\n- Honor `context.Context` for timeouts and cancellation.\n- Put data access behind clear interfaces that support testing.\n- Use structured logs without exposing sensitive data.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Ignoring error returns, including `_` for critical errors.\n- Using `panic` in ordinary request flows instead of controlled error handling.\n- Coupling handlers directly to concrete repository implementations without an abstraction.\n- Running unbounded or unpaginated queries in potentially large endpoints.\n- Introducing concurrency without adequate synchronization or race protection.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run `go test ./...` in the affected scope.\n- Run `go test -race ./...` when concurrency changes.\n- Run `go vet ./...` without blocking findings.\n- Run the applicable Go lint check, such as `golangci-lint`, without critical errors.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
